                "text": "**Analyzing Training Examples**\n\nI'm starting by carefully examining the training data. For example 1, I've noted the input points are at the corners, and the outputs seem related to the position of the point within the box. I'm exploring the relationship between input locations and their corresponding output values, focusing on the central points in particular.\n\n\n**Investigating Coordinate Patterns**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the spatial relationships within the examples. I've realized that the center point isn't always calculated the obvious way, with the examples I saw. I am looking more closely at the offset of all the points to the central point (3,5) in example 2, comparing that to the same offset found in example 1. Specifically I'm going through the numbers, and comparing to see if I am missing something.\n\n\n**Discovering Directional Relationships**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. Each point's position seems determined by its diagonal distance from a central point. For the points 4, 6, and 1, I can trace their diagonal rays back to the point (3,5) which serves as the origin. Now I'm exploring if this (3,5) point is the intersection of these rays. I think I'm onto something.\n\n\n**Confirming Center Coordinates**\n\nI have mathematically confirmed the central coordinates are located at (3,5) and not (5,3). I have tested Example 3 to show how the points are relative to the centre. I am now exploring what happens when the points go diagonally from (5,3) to (3,5). I'm seeing a consistent pattern.\n\n\n**Formulating General Rule**\n\nI've cracked the code! It seems there's a central intersection point, (R, C), from which all input points' positions are derived diagonally. Each input point is at (R + k_i * dr_i, C + k_i * dc_i), where (dr_i, dc_i) is (+-1, +-1) and k_i > 1. The output then places a \"5\" at (R, C).\nEach original color X is placed at (R + dr, C + dc). The original points are unchanged. This elegantly explains all the examples so far. I confirmed it with Example 1 and it works perfectly.\n\n\n",
